<p class='text'>I like this lockdown.</p>
<p class='text'>Obviously, it's not good that there are people out there suffering, even dying.  It's not good that people who need to work to earn money can't do so.  It's not good that we have criminally incompetent people in charge who couldn't organize a bun fight in a bakery and seem to think that it's OK to just deny anything is happening.  I was planning on going to New Zealand this spring, where they seem to have people in charge who believe in science, but I'm glad I didn't: I think I would be stuck there right now, unable to leave.</p>
<p class='text'>But, where I am at least (Pennsylvania), it's nice weather, I'm allowed outside every day, we're allowed to visit our son (and have him visit us) because we aren't mingling with anyone else and we've finally managed to sell our house.  We put in on the market and moved into a rented apartment on the very day (March 13) that the governor of Pennsylvania decided we were going into lockdown.  The real estate market was then closed down for a month, but we managed to offload it once they allowed transactions to open up again.  I'm not sure about the pandemic risks associated with that, but the important thing is that we have moved it on.  It's possible that if we'd waited, the prices would have gone up after lockdown is over, but possibly not.</p>
<p class='text'>I like this apartment.  It has one terrible flaw, which is the noise that the plumbing makes when our upstairs neighbour has a shower in the morning.  We certainly don't need an alarm clock!  But other than that, it's just about perfect: we were able to bring our lovely Amish-made table and it fits in the dining room.  I mean that pretty much literally, as it basically <i>fills</i> the dining room.  But it's a very good desk for working at, and the layout of the apartment means that we are generally in the same space, but my wife can watch TV or be in the kitchen while I'm working.</p>
<p class='text'>We can sit outside every afternoon once its warm enough, until the sun comes around and starts shining on the patio.  We can't go to coffee shops, but we can make milky drinks in the microwave (oftentimes using coconut milk since other milk products are in short supply).  From time to time our son makes brownies, even adding almonds to some of them to make faux-amaretto brownies a la Peet's Coffee.</p>
<p class='text'>We go out for a walk at least once a day too.  We can walk down past Starbucks and count the number of cars at the drive thru (only staff are allowed inside) and then walk around the mall car park.  From time to time we do see other people, but obviously we don't interact with them.</p>
<p class='text'>While I'm here I'd like to put a plug in for PhiloTV.   When we moved, I "cut the cord" and got rid of cable tv.  I wasn't sure how it would work out, but I knew pretty much what we watched, and we already had access to all the sports I wanted to watch, so that just left "entertainment tv".  Philo has a good range of those channels: Hallmark, ID, HGTV, etc. and it has lots of features I love such as "join at the start of any program", "watch replays" as well as "what's on now", not to mention the "continue watching" feature and an app design that is almost impossible to improve on, plus a fantastic Roku app.  In short, it's amazing.</p>
<p class='text'>The weather is lovely, we get out for walks, we can still have coffee, we can watch TV, I can get work done ... what's not to like?</p>
